#{[17592186498448 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498448 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498448 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498448 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186497916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498448 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b3ef4a65-1363-5281-b2e0-253a6eed11ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498520 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498520 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498520 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498520 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f77715c-0903-5f4b-a449-6816c6d9bb06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498520 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4t\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=7t\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(4t)={e}^{4t} \\\\ r(7t)={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t} \\\\ 7t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtract the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the diagonal entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}\\cdot t+\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{4t} \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}\\cdot t+\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498522 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336086" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498522 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498522 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the {{field-1}} of {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line {{field-4}} by the properties of invertible matrices." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3fdf52f0-09f2-54b2-864b-627c885837ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498522 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L1}.$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the $\\text{columns}$ of $Φ,$ namely the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ These functions are the fundamental solutions of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the properties of invertible matrices." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498584 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498584 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498584 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498584 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498584 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498584 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that every solution $x(t)$ can be written in the form $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c=[{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}]^{T}$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of solutions\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)$ can be written as $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)\\,c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-3}} by the transitive property of equality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498584 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d656b9e3-27c9-51a6-a70f-c9d56643be83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498584 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Once ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis, every solution $x(t)$ of the system can be expressed uniquely as a linear combination\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the definition of a basis}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are the columns of $Φ(t),$ we can write\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen by the definition of matrix multiplication,\n\n$$\nΦ(t)\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of matrix multiplication}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ In $\\text{L3}$ we have $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t),$ and in $\\text{L4}$ we have $Φ(t)c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).$ Since both expressions are equal to the same quantity, the transitive property of equality gives $x(t)=Φ(t)c.$ Thus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L3 and L4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498607 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186498653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498607 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0d32e49b-f44f-5aaf-b893-9c5777c8ec8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498607 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186311648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498619 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186498607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498619 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "297b5ab8-1639-57a8-8dcf-ef5dd67ae845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498619 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186498522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498620 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186498619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498620 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3ad30acf-66b9-5977-ad36-2183bb8c58c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498620 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186498520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498623 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186498620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498623 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3ea51f6a-6ea8-5162-ad6a-900689393007" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498623 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186365659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498653 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "db020f43-44ad-58e9-afc7-d835ef2b9210" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498653 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186498584 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
